IV. PREDICTIONS
We will first study the predictions of the RG procedure in some specific limits; see Sec. S20 for details. In Sec. S20.1 we demonstrate that the predicted critical temperature satisfies the lower-critical-dimension limit for the critical temperature, Tc → 0 for ς → ςlow [42]. In addition, Sec. S20.2 shows that, in the ferromagnetic limit, we recover the RG transformation of the ferromagnetic version of the HEA [33]. Finally, in Sec. S20.3 we show that at zero temperature and in the approximation where only first-level couplings are considered, our RG transformation reduces to an RG decimation procedure proposed recently [20]. We will now discuss the predictions of our RG method for the critical exponent ν related to the divergence of the correlation length [32]. We linearize the RG transformation at the critical fixed distribution; see Sec. S21 for details. Introducing the scaled quantiles Kk ≡ βLk if the Jacobian J of the scaled RG transformation Kk → Kk+1 has at least one eigenvalue with norm larger than unity, then ν is obtained from the eigenvalue λ with the largest norm by means of the relation λ = 21/ν [32]. In Fig. 2(a) we show the linearization of the RG transformation at the critical fixed distribution for ς = 0.8, obtained with N = 26 bins, and S = 212 and M = 222 as parameters for the numerical solution of the RG equations with stochastic-approximation methods; see Sec. S16. There is one relevant eigenvalue λ , implying that the critical fixed distribution is unstable. The eigenvectors relative to λ are shown in Fig. S10. The numerical value of ν as a function of ς is shown in Fig. 2(b), which constitutes one of the main results of this work. To test the prediction of the RG approach, we performed extensive Monte Carlo (MC) simulations for two diluted versions of the HEA, see Sec. S24 for details, whose predictions for ν are shown in Fig. 2(b). In addition, Fig. 2(b) shows values of ν from previous MC simulations for two one-dimensional SG models where the interaction strength decays with distance r as ∼r−ς [41,52]. The three simulated models above are supposed to belong to the same universality class as the HEA studied with the RG method [27,53].
